.SH NAME
Test2::V1 \- V1 edition of the Test2 recommended bundle.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This is the first sequel to Test2::V0. This module is recommended over
Test2::V0 for new tests.
.SS "Key differences from Test2::V0"
.IX Subsection "Key differences from Test2::V0"
.IP "Only 1 export by default: \fBT2()\fR" 4
.IX Item "Only 1 export by default: T2()"
.PD 0
.IP "No pragmas by default" 4
.IX Item "No pragmas by default"
.IP "srand and utf8 are not enabled by default" 4
.IX Item "srand and utf8 are not enabled by default"
.IP "Easy to still import everything" 4
.IX Item "Easy to still import everything"
.IP "East to still enable pragmas" 4
.IX Item "East to still enable pragmas"
.PD
.SH "NAMING, USING, DEPENDING"
.IX Header "NAMING, USING, DEPENDING"
This bundle should not change in a \fIseverely\fR incompatible way. Some minor
breaking changes, specially bugfixes, may be allowed. If breaking changes are
needed then a new \f(CW\*(C`Test2::V#\*(C'\fR module should be released instead.
.PP
Adding new optional exports, and new methods on the \fBT2()\fR handle are not
considered breaking changes, and are allowed without bumping the V# number.
Adding new plugin shortcuts is also allowed, but they cannot be added to the
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-P\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`\-plugins\*(C'\fR shortcuts without a bump in V# number.
.PP
As new \f(CW\*(C`V#\*(C'\fR modules are released old ones \fImay\fR be moved to different cpan
distributions. You should always use a specific bundle version and list that
version in your distributions testing requirements. You should never simply
list Test2::Suite as your modules dep, instead list the specific bundle, or
tools and plugins you use directly in your metadata.
.PP
See the "JUSTIFICATION" section for an explanation of why Test2::V1 was
created.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.SS RECOMMENDED
.IX Subsection "RECOMMENDED"
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-utf8;
\&
\&    T2\->ok(1, "pass");
\&
\&    T2\->is({1 => 1}, {1 => 1}, "Structures Match");
\&
\&    # Note that prototypes do not work in method form:
\&    my @foo = (1, 2, 3);
\&    T2\->is(scalar(@foo), 3, "Needed to force scalar context");
\&
\&    T2\->done_testing;
.Ve
.SS "WORK LIKE V0 DID"
.IX Subsection "WORK LIKE V0 DID"
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-ipP;
\&
\&    ok(1, "pass");
\&
\&    is({1 => 1}, {1 => 1}, "Structures Match");
\&
\&    my @foo = (1, 2, 3);
\&    is(@foo, 3, "Prototype forces @foo into scalar context");
\&
\&    # You still have access to T2
\&    T2\->ok(1, "Another Pass");
\&
\&    done_testing;
.Ve
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`\-ipP\*(C'\fR argument is short for \f(CW\*(C`\-include, \-pragmas, \-plugins\*(C'\fR which together enable all
pragmas, plugins, and import all symbols.
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR The order in which \f(CW\*(C`i\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`P\*(C'\fR appear is not important;
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-Ppi\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\-piP\*(C'\fR and any other order are all perfectly valid.
.SS "IMPORT ARGUMENT GUIDE"
.IX Subsection "IMPORT ARGUMENT GUIDE"
.ie n .IP """\-P"" or ""\-plugins""" 4
.el .IP "\f(CW\-P\fR or \f(CW\-plugins\fR" 4
.IX Item "-P or -plugins"
Shortcut to include the following plugins: Test2::Plugin::UTF8,
Test2::Plugin::SRand, Test2::Plugin::ExitSummary.
.ie n .IP """\-p"" or ""\-pragmas""" 4
.el .IP "\f(CW\-p\fR or \f(CW\-pragmas\fR" 4
.IX Item "-p or -pragmas"
Shortcut to enable the following pragmas: \f(CW\*(C`strict\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`warnings\*(C'\fR.
.ie n .IP """\-i"" or ""\-import""" 4
.el .IP "\f(CW\-i\fR or \f(CW\-import\fR" 4
.IX Item "-i or -import"
Shortcut to import all possible exports.
.ie n .IP """\-x""" 4
.el .IP \f(CW\-x\fR 4
.IX Item "-x"
Shortcut to import any sub that has \*(Aq&\*(Aq in its prototype, things like
\&\f(CW\*(C`dies { ... }\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`warns { ... }\*(C'\fR, etc.
.Sp
While these can be used in method form: \f(CW\*(C`T2\->dies(sub { ... })\*(C'\fR it is a
little less convenient than having them imported. \*(Aq\-x\*(Aq will import all of
these, and any added in the future or included via an \f(CW\*(C`\-include => ...\*(C'\fR
import argument.
.ie n .IP """\-ipP"", ""\-pPi"", ""\-pP"", ""\-Pix"", etc.." 4
.el .IP "\f(CW\-ipP\fR, \f(CW\-pPi\fR, \f(CW\-pP\fR, \f(CW\-Pix\fR, etc.." 4
.IX Item "-ipP, -pPi, -pP, -Pix, etc.."
The \f(CW\*(C`i\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`P\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR short options may all be grouped in any order
following a single dash.
.ie n .IP @EXPORT_LIST 4
.el .IP \f(CW@EXPORT_LIST\fR 4
.IX Item "@EXPORT_LIST"
Any arguments provided that are not prefixed with a \f(CW\*(C`\-\*(C'\fR will be assumed to be
export requests. If there is an exported sub by the given name it will be
imported into your namespace. If there is no such sub an exception will be
thrown.
.ie n .IP """!EXPORT_NAME""" 4
.el .IP \f(CW!EXPORT_NAME\fR 4
.IX Item "!EXPORT_NAME"
You can prefix an export name with \f(CW\*(C`!\*(C'\fR to exclude it at import time. This is
really only usedul when combined with \f(CW\*(C`\-import\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`\-i\*(C'\fR.
.ie n .IP """EXPORT_NAME => { \-as => ""ALT_NAME"" }""" 4
.el .IP "\f(CWEXPORT_NAME => { \-as => ""ALT_NAME"" }\fR" 4
.IX Item "EXPORT_NAME => { -as => ""ALT_NAME"" }"
.PD 0
.ie n .IP """EXPORT_NAME => { \-prefix => ""PREFIX_"" }""" 4
.el .IP "\f(CWEXPORT_NAME => { \-prefix => ""PREFIX_"" }\fR" 4
.IX Item "EXPORT_NAME => { -prefix => ""PREFIX_"" }"
.ie n .IP """EXPORT_NAME => { \-postfix => ""_POSTFIX"" }""" 4
.el .IP "\f(CWEXPORT_NAME => { \-postfix => ""_POSTFIX"" }\fR" 4
.IX Item "EXPORT_NAME => { -postfix => ""_POSTFIX"" }"
.PD
You may specify a hashref after an export name to rename it, or add a
prefix/postfix to the name.
.SS "RENAMING IMPORTS"
.IX Subsection "RENAMING IMPORTS"
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \*(Aq\-import\*(Aq, \*(Aq!ok\*(Aq, ok => {\-as => \*(Aqmy_ok\*(Aq};
.Ve
.PP
Explanation:
.IP \*(Aq\-import\*(Aq 4
.IX Item "'-import'"
Bring in ALL imports, no need to list them all by hand.
.IP \*(Aq!ok\*(Aq 4
.IX Item "'!ok'"
Do not import \f(CWok()\fR (remove it from the list added by \*(Aq\-import\*(Aq)
.IP "ok => {\-as => \*(Aqmy_ok\*(Aq}" 4
.IX Item "ok => {-as => 'my_ok'}"
Actually, go ahead and import \f(CWok()\fR but under the name \f(CWmy_ok()\fR.
.PP
If you did not add the \f(CW\*(Aq!ok\*(Aq\fR argument then you would have both \f(CWok()\fR and
\&\f(CWmy_ok()\fR
.SH "PRAGMAS AND PLUGINS"
.IX Header "PRAGMAS AND PLUGINS"
\&\fBNO PRAGMAS ARE ENABLED BY DEFAULT\fR
\&\fBONLY THE EXIT SUMMARY PLUGIN IS STILL ENABLED BY DEFAULT\fR
.PP
This is a significant departure from Test2::V0.
.PP
You can enable all of these with the \f(CW\*(C`\-pP\*(C'\fR argument, which is short for
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-plugins, \-pragmas\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`P\*(C'\fR is short for plugins, and \f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR is short for
pragmas. When using the single\-letter form they may both be together following
a single dash, and can be in any order. They may also be combined with \f(CW\*(C`i\*(C'\fR to
bring in all imports. \f(CW\*(C`\-p\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`\-P\*(C'\fR ont heir own are also perfectly valid.
.IP strict 4
.IX Item "strict"
You can enable this with any of these arguments: \f(CW\*(C`\-strict\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-p\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-pragmas\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
This enables strict for you.
.IP warnings 4
.IX Item "warnings"
You can enable this with any of these arguments: \f(CW\*(C`\-warnings\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-p\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-pragmas\*(C'\fR.
.Sp
This enables warnings for you.
.IP srand 4
.IX Item "srand"
You can enable this in multiple ways:
.Sp
.Vb 3
\&    use Test2::V1 \-srand
\&    use Test2::V1 \-P
\&    use Test2::V1 \-plugins
.Ve
.Sp
See Test2::Plugin::SRand.
.Sp
This will set the random seed to today\*(Aqs date.
.Sp
You can also set a random seed:
.Sp
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-srand => { seed => \*(Aqmy seed\*(Aq };
.Ve
.IP utf8 4
.IX Item "utf8"
You can enable this in multiple ways:
.Sp
.Vb 3
\&    use Test2::V1 \-utf8
\&    use Test2::V1 \-P
\&    use Test2::V1 \-plugins
.Ve
.Sp
See Test2::Plugin::UTF8.
.Sp
This will set the file, and all output handles (including formatter handles), to
utf8. This will turn on the utf8 pragma for the current scope.
.IP summary 4
.IX Item "summary"
This is turned on by default.
.Sp
You can avoid enabling it at import this way:
.Sp
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-summary => 0;
.Ve
.Sp
See Test2::Plugin::ExitSummary.
.Sp
This plugin has no configuration.
