.SH "THE \fBT2()\fP HANDLE"
.IX Header "THE T2() HANDLE"
The \f(CWT2()\fR subroutine imported into your namespace returns an instance of
Test2::V1::Handle. This gives you a handle on all the tools included by
default. It also creates a completely new namespace for use by your test that
can have additional tools added to it.
.SS "ADDING/OVERRIDING TOOLS IN YOUR T2 HANDLE"
.IX Subsection "ADDING/OVERRIDING TOOLS IN YOUR T2 HANDLE"
.Vb 7
\&    # Method 1
\&    use Test2::V1 T2 => {
\&        include => [
\&            [\*(AqTest2::Tools::MyTool\*(Aq, \*(Aqmy_tool\*(Aq, \*(Aqmy_other_tool\*(Aq],
\&            [\*(AqData::Dumper\*(Aq, \*(AqDumper\*(Aq],
\&        ],
\&    };
\&
\&    # Method 2
\&    use Test2::V1 T2 => {
\&        include => {
\&            \*(AqTest2::Tools::MyTool\*(Aq => [\*(Aqmy_tool\*(Aq, \*(Aqmy_other_tool\*(Aq],
\&            \*(AqData::Dumper\*(Aq         => \*(AqDumper\*(Aq,
\&        },
\&    };
\&
\&    # Method 3 (This also works with a hashref instead of an arrayref)
\&    use Test2::V1 \-include => [
\&        [\*(AqTest2::Tools::MyTool\*(Aq, \*(Aqmy_tool\*(Aq, \*(Aqmy_other_tool\*(Aq],
\&        [\*(AqData::Dumper\*(Aq, \*(AqDumper\*(Aq],
\&    ];
\&
\&    # Method 4
\&    T2\->include(\*(AqTest2::Tools::MyTool\*(Aq, \*(Aqmy_tool\*(Aq, \*(Aqmy_other_tool\*(Aq);
\&    T2\->include(\*(AqData::Dumper\*(Aq, \*(AqDumper\*(Aq);
\&
\&    # Using them:
\&
\&    T2\->my_tool(...);
\&
\&    T2\->Dumper({hi => \*(Aqthere\*(Aq});
.Ve
.PP
Note that you MAY override original tools such as \fBok()\fR, \fBnote()\fR, etc. by
importing different copies this way. The first time you do this there should be
no warnings or errors. If you pull in multiple tools of the same name an
redefine warning is likely.
.PP
This also effects exports:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-import, \-include => [\*(AqData::Dumper\*(Aq];
\&
\&    print Dumper("Dumper can be imported from your include!");
.Ve
.SS "OTHER HANDLE OPTIONS"
.IX Subsection "OTHER HANDLE OPTIONS"
.Vb 6
\&    use Test2::V1 T2 => {
\&        include   => $ARRAYREF_OR_HASHREF,
\&        namespace => $NAMESPACE,
\&        base      => $BASE_PACKAGE // \*(AqTest2::V1::Base\*(Aq,
\&        stomp     => $BOOL,
\&    };
.Ve
.ie n .IP "include => $ARRAYREF_OR_HASHREF" 4
.el .IP "include => \f(CW$ARRAYREF_OR_HASHREF\fR" 4
.IX Item "include => $ARRAYREF_OR_HASHREF"
See "ADDING TOOLS TO YOUR T2 HANDLE".
.ie n .IP "namespace => $NAMESPACE" 4
.el .IP "namespace => \f(CW$NAMESPACE\fR" 4
.IX Item "namespace => $NAMESPACE"
Normally a new namespace will be generated for you. You \fBCANNOT\fR rely on the
package name being anything specific unless you provide your own.
.Sp
The namespace here will be where any tools you \*(Aqinclude\*(Aq will be imported into.
It will also have its base class set to the base class you specify, or the
Test2::V1::Base module if you do not provide any.
.Sp
If this namespace already has any symbols defined in it an exception will be
thrown unless the \f(CW\*(C`stomp\*(C'\fR argument is set to true (not recommended).
.ie n .IP "stomp => $BOOL" 4
.el .IP "stomp => \f(CW$BOOL\fR" 4
.IX Item "stomp => $BOOL"
Used to allow the handle to stomp on an existing namespace (NOT RECOMMENDED).
.ie n .IP "base => $BASE" 4
.el .IP "base => \f(CW$BASE\fR" 4
.IX Item "base => $BASE"
Set the base class from which functions should be inherited. Normally this is
set to Test2::V1::Base.
.Sp
Another interesting use case is to have multiple handles that use eachothers
namespaces as base classes:
.Sp
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1;
\&
\&    use Test2::V1::Handle(
\&        \*(AqT3\*(Aq,
\&        base    => T2\->HANDLE_NAMESPACE,
\&        include => {\*(AqAlt::Ok\*(Aq => \*(Aqok\*(Aq};
\&    );
\&
\&    T3\->ok(1, "This uses ok() from Alt::Ok, but all other \-> methods are the original");
\&    T3\->done_testing(); # Uses the original done_testing
.Ve
.SH "EXAMPLE USE CASES"
.IX Header "EXAMPLE USE CASES"
.SS "OVERRIDING INCLUDED TOOLS WITH ALTERNATES"
.IX Subsection "OVERRIDING INCLUDED TOOLS WITH ALTERNATES"
Lets say you want to use the Test2::Warnings version of \f(CWwarning()\fR,
\&\f(CWwarnings()\fR instead of the Test2::Tools::Warnings versions, and also
wanted to import everything else Test2::Warnings provides.
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-import, \-include => [\*(AqTest2::Warnings\*(Aq];
.Ve
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`\-include => [\*(AqTest2::Warnings\*(Aq]\*(C'\fR option means we want to import the
default set of imports from Test2::Warnings into our \f(CWT2()\fR handle\*(Aqs
private namespace. This will override any methods that were also previously
defined by default.
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`\-import\*(C'\fR option means we want to import all subs into the current namespace.
This includes anything we got from Test2::Warnings, and we will get the
Test2::Warnings version of those subs.
.PP
.Vb 5
\&    like(
\&        warning { warn \*(Aqxxx\*(Aq }, # This is the Test2::Warnings version of \*(Aqwarning\*(Aq
\&        qr/xxx/,
\&        "Got expected warning"
\&    );
.Ve
.SH TOOLS
.IX Header "TOOLS"
.SS TARGET
.IX Subsection "TARGET"
\&\fIAdded to Test::V1 in 1.302217.\fR
.PP
See Test2::Tools::Target.
.PP
You can specify a target class with the \f(CW\*(C`\-target\*(C'\fR import argument. If you do
not provide a target then \f(CW$CLASS\fR and \f(CWCLASS()\fR will not be imported.
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-target => \*(AqMy::Class\*(Aq;
\&
\&    print $CLASS;  # My::Class
\&    print CLASS(); # My::Class
.Ve
.PP
Or you can specify names:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    use Test2::V1 \-target => { pkg => \*(AqSome::Package\*(Aq };
\&
\&    pkg()\->xxx; # Call \*(Aqxxx\*(Aq on Some::Package
\&    $pkg\->xxx;  # Same
.Ve
.ie n .IP $CLASS 4
.el .IP \f(CW$CLASS\fR 4
.IX Item "$CLASS"
Package variable that contains the target class name.
.ie n .IP "$class = \fBCLASS()\fR" 4
.el .IP "\f(CW$class\fR = \fBCLASS()\fR" 4
.IX Item "$class = CLASS()"
Constant function that returns the target class name.
